WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:08.960
SIERRA: on a scale of one to ten, how&nbsp;
strongly do you think we responded to 2016?
&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.960
NANCE: Zero.
SIERRA: Oh. That’s—I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.560
didn’t even have it on the scale.
NANCE: It’s not on the scale. It’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:21.520
on the scale. We’re not prepared at all for&nbsp;
2020. The way Russia had attacked us was using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.080
truths, half-truths, and lies as sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:33.440
information cruise missiles. And the air that they&nbsp;
glide on is the internet. The explosive payload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.200
is the lie that’s embedded in there. And the&nbsp;
problem is, unlike a regular cruise missile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.320
where the payload hits and explodes, this is&nbsp;
more like spreading a—you know, a virus. 
&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.760
SIERRA: Right, it’s like a rumor or a lie.&nbsp;
And once it’s out, even if you say it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.960
a lie or a rumor it can’t be put back.
NANCE: But it’s more insidious than a—just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:57.520
rumor or a lie. What was hacked in 2016 wasn’t&nbsp;
the Democratic National Committee; it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:06.800
mindset of the American voter. And the way they&nbsp;
hacked it was to enter it, pour in misinformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:14.320
allow you and your loved ones to spread the&nbsp;
virus. And now, completely infected, you refuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.469
to believe that anything is wrong with you. 
SIERRA: Well, that’s bleak and scary. (Laughs.)
&nbsp;

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:26.400
NANCE: (Laughs) I’m good at that.
SIERRA: Election talk can feel exhausting--hacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:33.040
and meddling and disinformation. But we have to&nbsp;
talk about it, because we got hacked in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:40.160
hard. Not just our voter databases, and not&nbsp;
just the DNC email servers, but... us. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.080
Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It Matters.&nbsp;
Today, how Russia’s been hacking democracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:55.680
and whether or not we’re ready for 2020.  
SIERRA: All right. So let’s pretend that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.440
was just frozen in a block of ice and I have&nbsp;
no idea what happened in the 2016 election.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.600
So what the heck happened in the 2016 election?
NANCE: Well, fundamentally what happened in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.320
2016 election is that the United States&nbsp;
carried out what we thought at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.080
was a free and fair election. 
SIERRA: This is Malcolm Nance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.600
a former Naval Intelligence officer and an&nbsp;
expert on national security and counterterrorism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:28.000
He’s also a regular contributor to MSNBC. 
NANCE: But as we were coming into the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:35.600
season in April and May of 2016, we were&nbsp;
discovering that there was an intrusion at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:42.000
Democratic National Committee’s computer servers,&nbsp;
and that this intrusion was carried out by two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.680
Russian intelligence entities. And what they&nbsp;
did was they penetrated the DNC’s servers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:53.164
stole all of their information, and&nbsp;
essentially carried out Watergate, but a—
&nbsp;

00:02:53.164 --> 00:02:56.800
SIERRA: Hmm. But a hacking Watergate.
NANCE: Right, a cyber Watergate. But why?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:06.880
So Russia apparently carried out this attack&nbsp;
in order to spread information about the DNC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:15.280
use the emails which came from their servers,&nbsp;
and then used WikiLeaks, led by Julian Assange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:20.560
gave them all of these emails, and then let&nbsp;
them put them into the information stream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.480
to essentially act like catnip for the global&nbsp;
media in order to damage Hillary Clinton.
&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.720
PBS NEWSHOUR: The CIA and other U.S.&nbsp;
intelligence agencies have concluded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.920
the Russian government was behind the email&nbsp;
hack into the Democractic National Committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.360
and other political organizations. 
CBS NEWS: Officials actually believe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.240
hacking of the DNC went on for more than&nbsp;
a year, starting back in June of 2015 as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.880
part of a wave of cyberattacks into American&nbsp;
political and government institutions.   
&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:55.280
SIERRA: Because of all the news coverage, it can&nbsp;
be easy to think the DNC hack was the main element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.200
of Russia’s election interference. But there&nbsp;
were two other major components. One, was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:08.720
spreading of disinformation on social media. 
ROSENBERGER: What they did was essentially a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.440
largely covert social media operation...
SIERRA: This is Laura Rosenberger. She’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.840
the director of the Alliance for Securing&nbsp;
Democracy and a senior fellow at The German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.640
Marshall Fund. 
ROSENBERGER:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.920
...in order to pit Americans against each other,&nbsp;
sow chaos, create a more polarized electorate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.440
and as the intelligence community assessment has&nbsp;
concluded, undermine support for Secretary Clinton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.720
and, support, President Trump's prospects.
SIERRA: The final piece, used alongside the DNC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.640
hack and the spread of disinformation,&nbsp;
was the hacking of voter registration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.040
databases. For this part we turned to&nbsp;
Joseph Marks who writes a newsletter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.560
on cybersecurity for the Washington Post.   
MARKS: The U.S. intelligence agencies would tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:56.960
you yes, Russia did interfere in the 2016 U.S.&nbsp;
election. That’s the conclusion of seventeen U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.600
intelligence agencies, of the Mueller report,&nbsp;
and of the Senate Intelligence Committee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.760
It was a multifaceted campaign. The major thing&nbsp;
they did from a cybersecurity angle was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:15.280
probe election systems probably across the country&nbsp;
but it was at least twenty-some states this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.360
noticed and recorded in. And it’s&nbsp;
probably all fifty, according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.160
the Senate Intelligence Committee and&nbsp;
other people who have looked at it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:30.240
They actually penetrated voter databases in&nbsp;
Illinois and may have penetrated systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.640
in a couple of counties in Florida. There’s no&nbsp;
evidence that they actually changed any votes or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.600
did anything once they got into those systems.
SIERRA: So even though they didn’t change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.080
anything, having the access is the problem?
MARKS: Yeah. You don’t want Russia to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:48.000
access to your voter registration data or&nbsp;
to internal parts of your voting systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.480
—there are a couple of problems with that.&nbsp;
One, they could do something if they chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.640
to. And number two, just the possibility&nbsp;
of them doing it makes it easier both for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.360
Russia or for people internally to&nbsp;
suggest that the result of the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.760
isn’t what it was supposed to be.
SIERRA: So that was their goal?
&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:11.440
MARKS: Yeah, it was part of a broader&nbsp;
goal to sow chaos, create dissension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.880
show up American democracy as not working&nbsp;
as well as we always claim it does.
&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.040
SIERRA: So they were super successful then.
MARKS: Yeah, in a lot of ways. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:27.120
all of the chaos and the partisanship that&nbsp;
we’ve experienced, and the lack of trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.560
that the election came out well, the anxiety about&nbsp;
2020, that’s exactly what Vladimir Putin wanted.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.680
SIERRA: Look, it’s impossible to say whether&nbsp;
or not Russia changed the outcome of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.800
2016 election. But what has changed is&nbsp;
the atmosphere surrounding elections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.840
Facts seem up for debate in a way that&nbsp;
they weren’t before. And this brings us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.320
disinformation. 
ROSENBERGER:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.120
So I think it's important to understand that&nbsp;
we use the term "disinformation" as a sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.320
of broad-sweeping, you know, all-encompassing&nbsp;
catchword. But in fact, the kind of things they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.440
did weren't necessarily all what's classically&nbsp;
defined as disinformation. So for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.040
the Russian social media manipulators were very&nbsp;
active on issues of race. Racism in our country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.280
is a real problem, and it's authentically&nbsp;
American, and it's a huge vulnerability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.640
and the Russians have taken advantage of that.&nbsp;
And so in 2016, and afterwards, what they did was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.480
you know, this was the time when the Black&nbsp;
Lives Matter movement was- was very active.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.960
They created a lot of fake accounts. 
SIERRA: One of those fake accounts portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.960
a fictional woman named Louisa Haynes. 
ROSENBERGER: ...her handle was “Woke Louisa”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.800
She was supposedly a young&nbsp;
African American activist from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.040
New York. She believed that Hillary&nbsp;
Clinton was a very bad candidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.040
on criminal justice issues, and thought that&nbsp;
African Americans shouldn't vote for her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:55.920
she was an account created by operatives in&nbsp;
Saint Petersburg at the Internet Research Agency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.920
But she actually not only spread&nbsp;
these views on social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:03.602
but actual traditional media outlets cited-
SIERRA: Oh my gosh. 
&nbsp;

00:08:03.602 --> 00:08:08.080
ROSENBERGER: ... quote, "Louisa Haynes"&nbsp;
as an example of African Americans who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.600
were concerned about Hillary Clinton's record on&nbsp;
criminal justice issues. And there's some great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.640
research that was done by a professor out the&nbsp;
University of Washington named Kate Starbird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.840
she found large clusters in both the Black Lives&nbsp;
Matter conversation and the Blue Lives Matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.880
conversation of these fake Russian accounts.&nbsp;
They were pretending to be real Americans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.880
talking about these issues. And she found&nbsp;
that those accounts were doing two things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.560
One, they were either the most active and&nbsp;
prolific- of those clusters, or they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.520
pushing the most extreme and most polarizing&nbsp;
kinds of perspectives on those issues. 
&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:44.880
SIERRA:
Wow. And playing both sides. 
&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.120
ROSENBERGER: Absolutely playing both sides. And&nbsp;
I could go down a litany of issues, where we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.520
the Russians, where we now know from the accounts&nbsp;
that have been released, that they were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.120
weighing in on both sides of these issues. 
SIERRA: Wow. 

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:02.240
SIERRA: Okay, Russia is good at stirring&nbsp;
the pot. But why mess with U.S. elections?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.960
What’s the goal?
ROSENBERGER:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:13.040
So, Russia is an objectively declining power.&nbsp;
And that means that for Vladimir Putin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:20.320
he can't really use a lot of conventional tools&nbsp;
and strategies in order to grow Russia's power on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:26.000
the world stage. For Putin, the things that matter&nbsp;
most are his own power, and, sort of, his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:32.400
security as a leader. And so, in order to actually&nbsp;
convince the Russian people that he's working to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:38.880
restore Russia's greatness, his best approach is&nbsp;
to weaken his competitors, and to use a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:45.920
of asymmetric tactics in order to undermine and&nbsp;
weaken those competitors from within. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.680
Putin has understood, that sort of weakening the&nbsp;
foundations of democracy is a way of attacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:58.880
democratic countries, and hindering our ability&nbsp;
to effectively govern, to effectively carry out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.840
our national interests by forcing us to, you&nbsp;
know, spend more time arguing with each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.920
which we're pretty good at by the way in the&nbsp;
first place. So at this point, sometimes it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.360
just accelerant to the fire. But I think really,&nbsp;
that's the long-term strategy, is- is weakening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.840
us from within.
NANCE:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:26.560
Now we are in a Russia that is led by a former&nbsp;
KGB officer. He has—he knows that his ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:33.280
is just money, but so is the Western ideology.&nbsp;
But he also has political goals that, in essence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:40.000
just means make Russia great again, right? And&nbsp;
to make Russia great, he clearly just looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.480
back at the Soviet model of how they spent&nbsp;
decades trying to destabilize the West and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:52.880
bring down liberal democracies. And the way the&nbsp;
Soviets tried to do it was, you know, subversion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.080
paying off the Communist parties of each of these&nbsp;
countries, on occasion buying a politician and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:03.200
trying to get them in a scandal. Putin realized&nbsp;
that those old methods were never going to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.560
right? What you do is that you work within&nbsp;
these democracies by creating political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:14.800
parties that are aligned with you, and you use&nbsp;
democracy to vote democracy out of existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:23.600
And so what we saw was, in concert with&nbsp;
this political ideology, he was using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:29.280
a new methodology on an old technique,&nbsp;
right? He was using information warfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:35.360
but disseminated through social media, which&nbsp;
was now moving at the speed of a keystroke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:46.080
It is literally an attempt to realign the poles of&nbsp;
the world as they have existed since World War II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:50.800
which is where Russia becomes a major&nbsp;
strong—asymmetric, though—player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:57.600
even though they are a very poor country, you&nbsp;
know, which is led by an oligarchy through a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.040
very severe autocrat.
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.720
What are some interference scenarios&nbsp;
that we should be looking out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.920
heading towards 2020 that maybe we didn't&nbsp;
see in 2016 or have escalated since 2016?
&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.560
ROSENBERGER: Yeah, so one of the things I'm&nbsp;
really concerned about is the hijacking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:22.000
activist's movements. I mentioned how we saw some&nbsp;
of this around particularly the Black Lives Matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:29.360
movement, before and after 2016. And that is one&nbsp;
thing I'm particularly concerned about for a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:35.840
reasons. One is that, by going after activists&nbsp;
you're often going after groups that feel already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:42.720
that they may be disenfranchised in some way&nbsp;
or another. And so by hijacking these movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:50.240
it can actually serve to delegitimize them, and&nbsp;
we actually saw this, for instance, in France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:56.560
where the Gilets Jaunes movement, the yellow&nbsp;
vests. The Russians not surprisingly sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:01.600
jumped on that movement, both on social&nbsp;
media and a little bit in the streets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:08.880
In order to, again, sort of discredit President&nbsp;
Macron, undermine, French democracy, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.120
just sow chaos, right?
SIERRA: Right, just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:14.800
drop and it poisons the well.
ROSENBERGER: Yeah, and to me that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.640
really dangerous because if activists are being&nbsp;
discredited just because the Russians hijacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:25.520
their movement, that, in certain scenarios,&nbsp;
can drive people even further to extremes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:30.960
those kinds of trends really, really worry&nbsp;
me. Another concern I have is that the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:38.000
cyber probes that we saw on election systems in&nbsp;
2016 could be repeated and could be potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:45.120
coupled with a disinformation campaign. So the&nbsp;
cyber probes don't actually change anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:52.640
but simply the fact of those probes&nbsp;
having occurred could be used to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:58.320
dispute the results of the election. 
SIERRA: This right here is the point. Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:04.080
you’ve been hacked, it’s hard to trust again.&nbsp;
The doubt sticks. A small cyber intrusion that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:09.280
doesn’t change a single vote might still be&nbsp;
enough to get Americans to argue about who won.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.840
And according to Joseph Marks, the hack wouldn’t&nbsp;
even have to hit voting infrastructure. 
&nbsp;

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:21.600
MARKS: If you get into an electric grid and affect&nbsp;
traffic lights in an urban area of a state but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:27.840
not the rural area of a state, and it’s a swing&nbsp;
state, that could, uh, drive up Republican votes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:33.200
drive down Democratic votes, and deliver that&nbsp;
state for the Republican candidate. You could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:40.480
shut down lights in a particular area that affects&nbsp;
the vote. You could do all sorts of things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:46.080
make it tougher for people to get out and vote.&nbsp;
And sow distrust in the system. And that also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:49.280
because we’ve been talking about the ways in&nbsp;
which Russia tries to play into divisions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:53.280
already exist, there’s a lot of concern&nbsp;
about voter suppression in this country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.280 --> 00:15:00.080
You could play into that in a way that makes&nbsp;
people less likely to trust the outcome of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:05.280
a particular race, whether it’s, you know,&nbsp;
congressional, presidential, or whatever.
&nbsp;

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:08.080
SIERRA: So the idea is that I’d&nbsp;
be stuck at a traffic light and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.687
wouldn’t get to the polls before they closed?
MARKS: Yes. The idea that multiple people-
&nbsp;

00:15:12.687 --> 00:15:14.560
SIERRA: Lots of people.
MARKS: -would be stuck at traffic lights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:19.680
decide that it’s not worth voting or create the&nbsp;
perception that a lot of people decided that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:26.080
And if that’s what happens in one area that trends&nbsp;
Democratic versus Republican, or vice versa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:31.760
that could lead into a narrative that had&nbsp;
things happened differently the outcome of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:35.040
the election would have been different. 
SIERRA: Just enough doubt to get people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:40.640
riled up and questioning the outcome. And if&nbsp;
it’s that easy to create division, how do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:45.360
ever recover? What can we do to unhack&nbsp;
ourselves and create a basis of trust? 
&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:52.400
MARKS: The number one thing that security experts&nbsp;
will tell you is paper ballots. Paper ballots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:58.400
or at the very least a paper backup that comes out&nbsp;
of the machine that the voter can look at and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:03.600
yes, this is what I intended. And then you put&nbsp;
that in lockbox, and it’s there to be counted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:08.080
afterwards. The second thing most people say&nbsp;
is risk-limiting audits, which is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:13.680
you look at the risks, you audit X&nbsp;
percent of ballots after the race to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:20.480
make sure that what people mark is lining up with&nbsp;
what the, um, computer reads from those ballots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:26.000
And if there are any irregularities then you&nbsp;
keep doing it until you’re doing a full hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:31.360
recount. That’s the lion’s share of what states&nbsp;
and localities have been doing since 2016. 
&nbsp;

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:35.040
SIERRA: So you’re saying, technology has failed&nbsp;
us. (Laughter.) We’re going back to paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:38.320
After that it’s raising our hands,&nbsp;
just nothing on the computer at all.
&nbsp;

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.520
MARKS: Yeah, it’s - it’s ironic because&nbsp;
2019 and paper’s the safest thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.960
but paper’s the safest thing.
SIERRA: But switching to paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:50.880
ballots isn’t the only step we could take.
ROSENBERG: We don't yet have robust enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:56.800
multilateral mechanisms to share information&nbsp;
either about threats and threat analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:01.280
or to share information about&nbsp;
responses, best practices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:06.320
lessons learned, what's working, what's not. 
SIERRA: So we gotta break down silos from the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:11.280
beginning, from the platforms, to the government,&nbsp;
all the way through to everybody across the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:13.600
talking about what's happening.
ROSENBERG: Yes. Breaking down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:18.400
silos and creating more integrated and&nbsp;
coordinated and collaborative approaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:23.440
underpin almost all of the responses, I think&nbsp;
we need to be taking to this problem set.
&nbsp;

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:31.680
NANCE: The only way to defeat this is for&nbsp;
democracy to truly reassert itself. The greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:36.960
disinfectant is truly sunlight. You have to talk&nbsp;
about it. You have to make it clear. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:42.720
only way you can do that is you really are—we are&nbsp;
really going to have to take democracy seriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:50.080
And I have no problem blowing the alarm. The&nbsp;
America as you know it and have known it and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:57.040
sat around and enjoyed for 243 years may transform&nbsp;
itself into an America you won’t recognize.
&nbsp;

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:03.120
SIERRA: So how prepared are we for 2020? 
ROSENBERGER: I think that we're certainly more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:08.080
aware of the threats- we've certainly&nbsp;
taken some steps. I would argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:13.520
none of that is sufficient. If anything the&nbsp;
country's just gotten more polarized since 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:20.400
The other thing, I would say on this, is that, you&nbsp;
know, candidly, this is a problem where we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:26.800
real strategic leadership from the top. And, we&nbsp;
need communication very clearly to the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:34.560
people about the threats that we see. And we have&nbsp;
not had that. And in fact, we've had what I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:40.080
are counterproductive messages on this, from&nbsp;
senior officials, including from the President,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:44.160
about, you know, whether it's&nbsp;
inviting other countries to interfere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:49.680
or the idea that accepting foreign help in an&nbsp;
election is not something that's problematic.
&nbsp;

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.760
SIERRA: Right.
ROSENBERGER: And frankly even casting doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:57.520
on whether Russia interfered, right? 
MARKS: You know, a lot of machines that did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:03.440
not have paper ballots or paper backups in 2016&nbsp;
will have them in 2020. The Homeland Security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:10.080
Department, which had visibility into just&nbsp;
a fraction of the digital happenings around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:16.560
election systems in 2016 will have&nbsp;
visibility into close to 100 percent of it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:22.320
2020. State and local election officials who&nbsp;
didn’t know a whole lot about cybersecurity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:29.920
before 2016 know a lot more about it now.&nbsp;
Those are all big and substantial changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:34.000
Have we done as much about disinformation? We’ve&nbsp;
done less. People are tracking it. They’re paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:37.360
a whole lot of attention to it. Social media&nbsp;
companies are paying a lot of attention to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:42.240
But there’s still certainly more to be done.&nbsp;
A point that some Homeland Security Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:47.600
officials sometimes make is part of this is just&nbsp;
about educating the public about what to expect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:54.240
And that’s really tough to do because we see&nbsp;
things on social media, and we react, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:58.320
it’s tough to shift people’s perspective&nbsp;
once they think something. Chaos is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:01.200
something we’re going to have to live with.&nbsp;
I mean, if you look at the last three years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:07.200
Putin got what he wanted in a number of cases.&nbsp;
Our trust in the system is not going to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:12.480
automatically on, you know, mid-November&nbsp;
of 2020. It’s going to take some time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:20.480
regain trust in the system, if we ever do.
SIERRA: Looking ahead, not just to 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:26.960
but to the future state of our democracy can be...&nbsp;
rough. New norms are being established every day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:32.160
as technology races ahead and we struggle&nbsp;
to understand how our world has changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:37.280
2016 can feel like old news, but it’s only&nbsp;
been a few years since this all went down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:41.360
It’s still a fresh bruise. And the&nbsp;
attacks are going to keep coming.   
&nbsp;

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:49.280
ROSENBERGER: So my hope, and trying to be&nbsp;
positive on this topic is sometimes hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:56.560
but my hope is that one of the things it taught&nbsp;
the world is that democracy is something we can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.560 --> 00:21:01.600
take for granted. I think that is something&nbsp;
we have been doing for a little bit too long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:07.120
especially in democracies that had been considered&nbsp;
to be consolidated. We had this idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:14.320
democracy would be just self-perpetuating. And&nbsp;
we stopped investing in some of the really core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:17.800
aspects of our democracy.
